egin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  \\\\ -\\pi \\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=-1,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-(-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  \\\\ -\\pi \\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n-1+(-1)+0=-2.\n$$" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi  \\\\ -\\pi \\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=-1,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-(-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi  \\\\ -\\pi \\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n-1+(-1)+0=-2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367783 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?"] [:db/cas 17592186367783 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1})=-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1})=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=0,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-0=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n0+0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1})=-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1})=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=0,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n0+0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367790 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{xy+1},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{xy+1},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?"] [:db/cas 17592186367790 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xy+1})=-\\frac{y}{(xy+1)^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{xy+1})=-\\frac{x}{(xy+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{(xy+1)^{2}} & -\\frac{x}{(xy+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-\\frac{1}{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4})+(-\\frac{1}{4})=0.\n$$" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xy+1})=-\\frac{y}{(xy+1)^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{xy+1})=-\\frac{x}{(xy+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{y}{(xy+1)^{2}} & -\\frac{x}{(xy+1)^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-\\frac{1}{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4})+(-\\frac{1}{4})=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367797 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at  $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4} \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & -1\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}]),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4} \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & -1\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{bmatrix}),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?"] [:db/cas 17592186367797 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2})=2{x}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2{x}_{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\pi  \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\pi  \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n0+\\pi +(-1)=\\pi -1.\n$$" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2})=2{x}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2{x}_{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\pi  \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\pi  \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n0+\\pi +(-1)=\\pi -1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367804 :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{yz} \\\\ {x}^{2}y{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}z} &  \\\\ 2xy{z}^{2} &  & 2{x}^{2}yz\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing entries?" "For the transformation $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x}{yz} \\\\ {x}^{2}y{z}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}z} &  \\\\ 2xy{z}^{2} &  & 2{x}^{2}yz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing entries?"] [:db/cas 17592186367804 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{yz})=\\frac{1}{yz},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{yz})=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}z},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{x}{yz})=-\\frac{x}{y{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y{z}^{2})=2xy{z}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y{z}^{2})={x}^{2}{z}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y{z}^{2})=2{x}^{2}yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{yz} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}z} & -\\frac{x}{y{z}^{2}} \\\\ 2xy{z}^{2} & {x}^{2}{z}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}yz\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{yz})=\\frac{1}{yz},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{yz})=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}z},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{x}{yz})=-\\frac{x}{y{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y{z}^{2})=2xy{z}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y{z}^{2})={x}^{2}{z}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y{z}^{2})=2{x}^{2}yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{yz} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}z} & -\\frac{x}{y{z}^{2}} \\\\ 2xy{z}^{2} & {x}^{2}{z}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}yz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367811 :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x-y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & ∗ \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x-y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & ∗ \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions."] [:db/cas 17592186367811 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+{e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2+{e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y} \\\\ -{e}^{x-y} & {e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-1)+(-1)+(x-1)=2x-3.\n$$" "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x+{e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+{e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y} \\\\ -{e}^{x-y} & {e}^{x-y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-1)+(-1)+(x-1)=2x-3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367818 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & -1\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & -1\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?"] [:db/cas 17592186367818 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2}^{2})=3{x}_{1}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2}^{2})=-2{x}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2})={x}_{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2})={x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2} & -2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n-5+4+(-2)=-3.\n$$" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2}^{2})=3{x}_{1}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2}^{2})=-2{x}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2})={x}_{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2})={x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{1}^{2} & -2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n-5+4+(-2)=-3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367825 :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}\\sqrt{y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}\\sqrt{y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions."] [:db/cas 17592186367825 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y} & \\frac{{x}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}6x\\sqrt{y} & \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y}} & -\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(2,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}12 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-2)+12+6=x+16.\n$$" "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y} & \\frac{{x}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}6x\\sqrt{y} & \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y}} & -\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(2,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-2)+12+6=x+16.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367832 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}}\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?"] [:db/cas 17592186367832 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2})=3{x}_{1}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2})=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}})={x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}})={e}^{{x}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2} & -1 \\\\ {x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}} & {e}^{{x}_{1}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n1+(-1)+1=1.\n$$" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2})=3{x}_{1}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2})=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}})={x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}})={e}^{{x}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{1}^{2} & -1 \\\\ {x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}} & {e}^{{x}_{1}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n1+(-1)+1=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367874 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ what type of point is $a?$" "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ what type of point is $a?$"] [:db/cas 17592186367881 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈3+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-7$\n- **II.** $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a saddle point of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈3+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-7$\n- **II.** $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a saddle point of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186367888 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-7+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** $A$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a local maximum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-7+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** $A$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a local maximum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186367901 :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\pi ,1,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(\\pi ,1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(\\pi ,1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(\\pi ,0,1).$" "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\pi ,1,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(\\pi ,1,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(\\pi ,1,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi  \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(\\pi ,0,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186367901 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-\\pi  \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-\\pi  \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-\\pi  \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\pi ,0,1) & ≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\pi -\\pi  \\\\ 0-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}\\pi -\\pi  \\\\ 0-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\pi -\\pi  \\\\ 0-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+1+\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}(3) \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi  \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\pi  \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\pi  \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\pi  \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi  \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\pi ,0,1) & ≈-1+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi -\\pi  \\\\ 0-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi -\\pi  \\\\ 0-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi -\\pi  \\\\ 0-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1+\\pi \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1+1+\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}(3) \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367908 :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,0) & =1+e, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(2,2,2).$" "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,0) & =1+e, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,1,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(1,1,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(2,2,2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186367908 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =1+e, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+e+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2,2) & ≈1+e+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}2-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1+e+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1+e+6+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7+e+\\frac{1}{2}(14) \\\\  & =14+e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =1+e, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+e+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2,2) & ≈1+e+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}2-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1+e+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1+e+6+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =7+e+\\frac{1}{2}(14) \\\\  & =14+e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367935 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3 & -10 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 & -12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3 & -10 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3 & -10 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 & -12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3 & -10 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367937 :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186367938 :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186367939 :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186367940 :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186367941 :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186367942 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y=3 \\\\ x-2y=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-7)}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y=3 \\\\ x-2y=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-7)}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367953 :answer/value "x=-[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "x=-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"]